\begin{didyouknow}{Gram, Schmidt, and Laplace}
The Gram-Schmidt procedure turns any set of independent vectors into an orthonormal basis by subtracting projections one at a time. It is named after J{\o}rgen Gram (1883) and Erhard Schmidt (1907), but Pierre-Simon Laplace had used an equivalent method decades earlier. Schmidt applied it to functions, which is exactly the infinite-dimensional setting of quantum mechanics.
\end{didyouknow}
% DYK-END

