\begin{beyond}
\textbf{Taken on trust: the volume interpretation.} The vector $\bm r_2\times\bm r_3$ is perpendicular to both $\bm r_2$ and $\bm r_3$, and its length is the area of the parallelogram they span. Dotting it with $\bm r_1$ therefore multiplies that base area by the height of $\bm r_1$ above the base (up to sign). Base times height is volume, so $\abs{\det A}=\abs{\bm r_1\cdot(\bm r_2\times\bm r_3)}$ is the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by the rows. By property (D7) below, $\det A^\top=\det A$, so it is also the volume spanned by the columns, which are the images $A\bm e_1,A\bm e_2,A\bm e_3$ of the edges of the unit cube. Hence $\abs{\det A}$ is the factor by which $A$ scales volumes. Proofs: Riley, Hobson and Bence \cite{rhb}, Chs.~7 and~8.
\end{beyond}

